Thus far we have treated our simple free list as a conceptual entity; it is just a list describing the free chunks of memory in the heap. But how do we build such a list inside the free space itself?


In a more typical list, when allocating a new node, you would just call malloc() when you need space for the node. Unfortunately, within the memory-allocation library, you can't do this! Instead, you need to build the list _inside_ the free space itself. Don't worry if this sounds a little weird; it is, but not so weird that you can't do it!


Assume we have a 4096-byte chunk of memory to manage (i. e. , the heap is 4KB). To manage this as a free list, we first have to initialize said list; initially, the list should have one entry, of size 4096 (minus the header size).


